%U A401479 45,45,46,46,47,47,48,49,50,51,51,52,53,54,55,56,57,58,58,58,59,60,61
%N A401479 a(n) is the size of the largest Sidon subset of the first n positive cubes.
%C A401479 A Sidon set is a set in which all sums of two elements, not necessarily distinct, are different.
%C A401479 Since x^3 + y^3 = 2*z^3 has no solutions in positive integers with x != y, two different sums of two cubes can be equal only when four different numbers are involved, as in 1^3 + 12^3 = 9^3 + 10^3 = 1729. So a(n) is n minus the least number of elements of {1, ..., n} that must be removed so that no equation a^3 + b^3 = c^3 + d^3 with {a, b} != {c, d} remains.
%C A401479 Erdős asked whether a(n) >> n.
%C A401479 For n <= 300, a(n) is attained by a Sidon subset that contains n^3, except at n = 80, 108, 156 and 238, where the largest Sidon subset containing n^3 has a(n) - 1 elements.
%H A401479 Leandre Jack, <a href="/A401479/b401479.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..300</a>
%H A401479 Thomas Bloom, <a href="https://www.erdosproblems.com/1206">Problem #1206</a>, Erdős Problems.
%H A401479 Leandre Jack, <a href="https://github.com/leandrejack-afk/erdos-computations/blob/main/e1206/README.md">Erdős Problem 1206: the largest Sidon subset of the first n cubes</a>, GitHub.
%e A401479 a(12) = 11: since 1^3 + 12^3 = 9^3 + 10^3 = 1729, one of 1, 9, 10 and 12 must be left out, and {1, ..., 11} works.
%Y A401479 Cf. A000578 (cubes), A001235 (taxicab numbers), A062292 (greedy Sidon sequence of cubes), A390813 (squares).
%K A401479 nonn,new
